{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a crucial role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a tumultuous era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His early life involved captivity following the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a ransom payment. This long imprisonment deeply influenced his political views and made him cautious about engaging in reckless military campaigns against Muslim forces. He later acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during certain periods of his reign. Raymond sought diplomatic solutions rather than immediate war because he understood the strength of the enemy armies surrounding the Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nHis capture occurred when he was still young and this experience shaped his entire approach to governance and warfare. The years spent in confinement taught him the language and customs of his captors while also revealing the internal divisions within Muslim territories that could be exploited through negotiation instead of brute force. Upon release he returned to Tripoli with a mature perspective that often clashed with more aggressive factions among the Latin nobility who favored constant raiding and expansion despite limited resources.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond assumed the regency for the young king Baldwin IV and this position placed him at the center of intense court intrigue involving various noble families vying for influence. He opposed the marriage of Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan because he feared Guy lacked the judgment needed to lead the kingdom during such dangerous times. His opposition created deep rifts within the royal court and led to factions forming around different leaders which ultimately weakened the unified defense of the realm against external threats.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nThe count retired to his own lands after losing influence at court yet he remained a respected figure among many barons who shared his cautious outlook. He died shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the forces he warned against finally crushed the Crusader army and led to the fall of Jerusalem. His death removed a vital voice of reason from the political landscape and his absence contributed significantly to the collapse of Crusader power in the Holy Land during the late twelfth century. Historians view him as a pragmatic leader whose strategies might have preserved the kingdom longer had they been fully adopted by his peers. His life story illustrates the complex challenges faced by Crusader lords trying to maintain their holdings amidst overwhelming odds and internal discord.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004940061}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0023330823}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.18271562}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.8266913e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0009228672}, {"token": " play